WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.025
An AI tool flags students as at risk. Before you trust it, ask five questions.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.900
Who was it tested on? And how accurate is it for each group, not just overall?

3
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:16.417
What kinds of errors happen? Take an AI text detector.

4
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:23.800
A false positive says your own writing was made by AI. A false negative misses text that really was.

5
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:34.567
Detectors have been shown to flag writing by non-native English speakers more often. So the cost of false positives falls on some students more than others.

6
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:43.933
Then ask who pays for the errors, and who checks the decision. Real oversight means a person who can override the tool, and is accountable.

7
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.275
If you cannot find these answers, that is a finding too.

8
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.725
With the pen: question one comes first.

9
00:00:53.175 --> 00:00:56.000
Two kinds of error, with different costs.

10
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:02.700
And real oversight has three parts: authority, the power to override, and accountability.
